# Line-ending policy
#
# Source files are LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, including on
# Windows. Without this, a Windows clone with `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites every
# checked-out file to CRLF, and Prettier's `endOfLine: "lf"` then reports the
# entire tree as unformatted even though nothing changed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Test fixtures are byte-exact inputs, not source code.
#
# Their line endings, byte-order marks, and deliberately invalid byte sequences
# are the behaviour under test, so Git must never normalize them:
#   - 08b-encoding-bom-crlf.csv is CRLF with a UTF-8 BOM *on purpose*
#   - 08c-invalid-utf8.csv carries a byte that is not valid UTF-8
# `-text` disables all conversion and keeps every fixture byte-for-byte.
tests/fixtures/**/*.csv -text

# Generated lockfile: keep it LF, and don't try to merge it by hand.
package-lock.json text eol=lf -merge

# Binary assets. None are tracked today, but the design system defers a
# self-hosted typeface (privacy-model.md §2 forbids remote fonts), so these
# arrive later and must never be line-ending converted.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
